WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
Let’s talk about postal voting and why it’s such a big deal

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.360
 in the US presidential election.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:12.200
A lot of voters want to mail in their ballots because of the pandemic — to stay safe.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.120
But President Donald Trump doesn’t like it.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:25.040
He’s being accused of trying to mess with the election.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.840
So how does postal voting work? Can it really lead to voter fraud?

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.640
And how might all of this affect the presidential election?

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:54.560
It’s pretty astonishing really that voting by mail — a pretty standard procedure around the world

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.280
is now one of the most talked about issues in this presidential race.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:02.720
We’ll get into all the politics in a second. But first here are some basics.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.640
Mail-in balloting in the US is pretty popular. 

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.720
It’s for voters who can’t get to a polling station on Election Day. 

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.760
It’s for the elderly, the sick, people who live abroad, military personnel

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:18.240
and people who just prefer to vote that way.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.840
President Trump mailed in his ballot in 2018.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.840
So did one-quarter of all voters in the last election.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.160
Different states have different rules.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:34.440
There’s what’s called universal mail voting where ballots are automatically sent to everyone.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:41.160
And this year five states will run mail-in-only elections — including Utah, a solidly Republican state.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:45.600
But in most states Americans have to apply to get what’s called an absentee ballot. 

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.920
They have to contact their local election authority 

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:51.960
and normally they have to make the case why they can’t vote in person.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:56.760
But this year because of the pandemic two-thirds of US states say they don’t require

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:01.280
registered voters to give an excuse in order to get an absentee ballot.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:05.360
Meaning a lot more people will be able to vote by mail this time around.

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:12.560
So understandably there’s concern that there’s going to be a strain on the United States Postal Service

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.080
that could lead to delays and mistakes. Because they do happen.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:24.120
In 2016 states rejected about 1% of ballots for things like late arrivals and missing signatures.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:35.080
But there are safeguards to try to reduce errors and stop fraud.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:40.640
For example an absentee ballot can only be sent to a person’s registered address.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.440
One envelope keeps the ballot private and a second envelope is used to mail it back

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:49.440
though most Americans choose to drop off the envelopes at government offices.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.680
Voters also have to sign the ballot envelope and election officials verify the signature.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.600
They also come with barcodes so they can be traced.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.440
All of those checks take extra time and money.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.640
But this year the US Postal Service is spending less — not more.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.160
There’ve been huge cuts. In some states it’s already led to delays.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:30.920
The man behind those cuts is the new postmaster general who took charge in June.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:36.160
Louis DeJoy is a businessman who was appointed by Trump to reform the Postal Service.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:37.920
And it is in trouble.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.800
It’s $160 billion in debt.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:47.800
That’s mostly because back in 2006 a new law forced the post office to prepay employees’ retirement

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:51.000
and healthcare benefits 75 years into the future.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:55.640
Add to that things like people not mailing as much as they used to. 

00:03:55.640 --> 00:04:01.040
But Trump’s critics are still sceptical of DeJoy’s appointment for a few reasons.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:08.160
He’s personally given the Republican Party and Donald Trump close to $3 million in donations since 2016.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:47.960
Another concern is about timing. Why bring these cuts in now?

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:00.040
So why are people suspicious?

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:03.920
For the last year President Trump has been attacking mail-in balloting.

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:06.520
And he’s making a lot of accusations.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:11.280
That ballots will be “forged &amp; even illegally printed out &amp; fraudulently signed.”

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:17.480
That mail drop boxes make it possible for people to vote multiple times
and that they aren’t COVID sanitised.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:21.640
That last one was actually flagged up by Twitter as potentially misleading.

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:28.560
- I think mail-in voting is horrible.
- But you voted by mail in Florida's election last month didn't you?

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.000
- Sure I can vote by mail.
- So how do you reconcile that?

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:32.000
- Because I’m allowed to.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.200
He's also openly said that universal mail-in voting — in his words

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:38.920
 “doesn’t work out well for Republicans.”

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:42.440
The Democrats are doing it because in theory it’s good for them.

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:03.360
At the same time postal voting is also used a lot by elderly voters.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:05.280
They tend to skew Republican.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:11.640
And Trump is actually encouraging mail-in voting in Florida which is home to a lot of retirees.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:13.480
But it’s a different story in Nevada.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:19.240
Trump is suing the state for changing its rules so that all registered voters get a ballot in the mail.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:24.040
Here’s the thing. Florida has a Republican governor. Nevada’s is a Democrat.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:27.680
It’s why Trump’s opponents feel this is all deeply political.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:31.720
OK so what about these allegations of possible corruption?

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:35.040
Well fraud is rare — but it does happen.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:40.160
The Washington Post did an analysis of recent elections in three vote-by-mail states.

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:46.880
They found 372 possible cases of double voting or voting on behalf of people who died.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:52.080
That was out of more than 14 million mail-in ballots. That’s a miniscule percentage.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.000
The most recent example of fraud was in 2018

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.440
involving a staff member of North Carolina’s Republican Congressman Mark Harris.

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:07.480
"Harris' political strategist McCrae Dowless, accused of tampering with more than 500 absentee ballots to help Harris win."

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:11.480
So while the politicians argue about the odds of a rigged election

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:15.880
plenty of people are worried that this will all just end up a giant mess.

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:19.920
And that’s mainly because of those budget cuts to the Postal Service.

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:28.880
DeJoy says he’s paused them. 
But some think the damage has already been done.

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:36.000
He was even called in by a congressional committee to reassure Americans that the postal system can handle all those votes.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.440
Many Democrats though aren’t convinced.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:53.040
The House of Representatives, which is controlled by the Democrats, passed an emergency bill to give $25 billion to the post office.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:55.880
More than two dozen Republicans voted for it.

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:01.800
But the Republican-controlled Senate hasn’t passed the bill.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:08.840
Anybody who wants to give money to the post office you might as well just put it into a big pile on your front lawn and burn it.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:11.880
You can't run a business with 80% of your cost being labour.

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:17.280
But politics aside … actually most Americans, Democrats and Republicans

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:21.240
hold up the Postal Service as an important public institution.

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:28.080
And right now it’s under a lot of pressure. It’s got to help deliver this election in the middle of a pandemic.

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:33.480
And delays are likely. Some states will still be counting votes that arrive after Election Day.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:38.360
So let’s be clear. We probably can’t expect to have a winner straightaway.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:43.400
And any hiccups could be used by both presidential candidates to contest the result.

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:45.760
And they say they’re ready for a fight.

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:07.800
This year more than ever Americans are relying on the US Postal Service to help choose their next president.

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:12.040
But this whole drama has created doubt in a system many have trusted.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:17.680
They need it to work. Because this election? It’s expected to be a close one.

